+# Homepage paint monitoring
+
+`performance-monitor.js` runs as a deferred, dependency-free script. It does not
+intercept links, change navigation, send network requests, or collect account data.
+
+Read `window.dailyGrindPerformance.capabilities` to see which observers registered.
+Unsupported performance entry types are skipped; unavailable metrics are not
+replaced with misleading timer or next-frame estimates.
+
+Consume reports locally or connect an analytics listener:
+
+```js
+window.addEventListener('daily-grind:performance', ({ detail }) => {
+  console.log(detail);
+});
+// Bounded history of the latest 100 reports:
+window.dailyGrindPerformance.getReports();
+```
+
+- `interaction-paint`: `duration` is milliseconds from the initiating interaction
+  to presentation of its contentful paint, including attributed asynchronous work.
+  Each progressively larger contentful paint emits a report. `control` identifies
+  `navigation`, `order-now`, or `seasonal-favorite`; labels distinguish links/cards.
+  Other or unattributable interactions have `control: 'other'`.
+- `soft-navigation`: `fcpDuration` and `lcpDuration` are milliseconds relative to
+  the initiating interaction, not to the original page load. Initial LCP includes
+  content painted before the URL update. Subsequent paints are joined by
+  `interactionId`, so unrelated interactions cannot update the route's LCP.
+  Reports with `final: false` are candidates; `final: true` finalizes at the next
+  soft navigation, page hide, or explicit `dailyGrindPerformance.flush()`.
+  Consumers should upsert by navigation ID, not count candidates as separate routes.
+
+These APIs report contentful paint candidates, not completion of every animation
+or a guarantee that all asynchronous work has finished. No paint means no paint
+report. Client-side view switches without a URL change can emit interaction paints
+but do not qualify as soft navigations. The current homepage links perform full
+document navigations: destination-page rendering requires monitoring on that page
+and is not measured by observers in the departing document.
+
+Input metadata uses Event Timing interaction IDs where available, with an exact
+input-start timestamp fallback for fast interactions. History and attribution
+buffers are capped at 100 entries. No DOM nodes are retained in reports.
+
+Run the observer logic checks without a browser or server:
+
+```sh
+node --test performance-monitor.test.cjs
+```
