+Client-side performance monitoring loads with the homepage, without changing link behavior.
+
+It measures primary, same-tab clicks on navigation links, Order Now, and Seasonal
+Favorites cards (including their View Details links). Keyboard-generated clicks
+are included. Modified clicks, downloads, and links opening another tab are ignored.
+
+Read the most recent 100 samples with `window.dailyGrindPerformance.getSamples()`.
+Each sample includes `type`, `label`, `startTime`, `duration` (milliseconds), and
+`source`. Samples also appear as `daily-grind:*` User Timing measures and are
+dispatched through the `daily-grind:performance` window event for a future analytics
+consumer. No data is sent to a server.
+
+Click samples use browser Event Timing when available (input timestamp through
+next paint, rounded by the browser). Fast clicks and unsupported browsers fall
+back to two animation frames. The fallback is explicitly labeled
+`animation-frame-estimate`: it measures a painting opportunity, not guaranteed
+display completion or the end of CSS transitions, image loading, or later async
+work. A 200 ms reporting grace period lets Event Timing replace the estimate;
+that grace period is excluded from the measured duration.
+
+History `pushState` / `replaceState`, `popstate`, and `hashchange` automatically
+measure the next painting opportunity after synchronous view changes. For async
+or state-only view switches, wrap the update and resolve it after the DOM commits:
+
+```js
+await window.dailyGrindPerformance.measureViewSwitch('menu', async () => {
+  const menu = await loadMenu();
+  renderMenu(menu);
+});
+```
+
+Explicit view measurements include async work and use the frame estimate. A
+router that also updates history can produce both a history sample and an
+explicit sample; their labels identify their different boundaries.
+
+Backgrounded or unloaded pages cancel pending measurements. Full document
+navigations cannot measure destination rendering from this homepage; a click
+sample, if completed before unloading, only describes this document's next paint.
