 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.

+## Developer diagnostics
+
+The homepage includes two independent browser-side connection checks:
+
+- Hardware test device: defaults to `http://192.168.1.100:8080/api/status`; accepts private LAN IPs and `.local` hostnames.
+- Companion CLI: defaults to `http://127.0.0.1:45678/health`; accepts loopback IPs and `localhost` hostnames.
+
+Replace each example URL with the service's actual read-only status endpoint. Serve the site over HTTPS (a trustworthy localhost development context is also permitted by browsers). No local requests run automatically: clicking Connect sends a single credential-free CORS GET directly from the reader's browser, with `targetAddressSpace` set to `local` or `loopback`. Redirects are rejected. A successful 2xx response records reachability and latency; this is not a persistent session, and response bodies are neither retained nor displayed. Requests can be cancelled and time out after 30 seconds.
+
+The device and CLI must be running and return standard CORS headers, such as `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: https://your-news-host.example` (the origin has no path). Browser Local network and Loopback network permissions are separate from CORS. Denied permissions must be enabled in browser site settings. Unsupported permission queries fall back to an explicit connection attempt; browsers without HTTP local-network support may still block that request. No deprecated Private Network Access preflight headers or server-side proxy are used.
+

 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
