+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This static app includes a two-origin migration package. No live origins were supplied; the default build uses the reserved examples `https://app.dailygrind.example` and `https://new.dailygrind.example`. These are placeholders, not deployed addresses.
+
+Build with your actual origins:
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
+OLD_APP_ORIGIN=https://app.example.com NEW_APP_ORIGIN=https://new.example.com pnpm build
+```
+
+Deploy the **contents** of `dist/new` to the new origin first, then the contents of `dist/old` to the old origin. Both must use HTTPS and share a registrable domain. The build validates this with a Public Suffix List, including private suffixes. Keep the old origin accessible during the transition. Do not redirect its manifest, settings transfer page, or `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association`. Serve the extensionless association file as JSON (HTTP 200), not as an HTML fallback. Serve `.webmanifest` as `application/manifest+json`.
+
+The old manifest advertises `migrate_to`; the new manifest declares `migrate_from`; the old association file authorizes exactly the new app ID. Identity is `/` at each origin. This repository had no previous manifest, so this assumes an existing installation identified by its root start URL. If your deployed installation has a different identity, preserve its actual manifest ID and start URL in `scripts/build.mjs` before rollout. Keep any existing production icon assets and manifest shortcuts there as well.
+
+Supporting browsers offer an installation update that preserves OS integration. Migration is not forced. Firefox, Safari, and other unsupported browsers need manual installation at the new origin; the page explains this fallback. Browser permissions, cookies, login sessions, and IndexedDB are not copied by this application. Users may need to sign in and grant permissions again. No browser support claim should be inferred from the presence of manifest fields alone.
+
+Settings transfer is separate from installation migration. On the old page, “Transfer settings and open new app” opens the destination and transfers the app-owned localStorage keys `daily-grind:settings` and `daily-grind:preferences`. This static page previously stored no settings; these are the explicit configuration namespace for integrations. Add any actual deployed configuration keys to `settingsKeys` in the build script; do not include authentication secrets or unrelated storage. Matching destination settings are replaced; unrelated destination keys and all source data are retained. No application data is deleted on migration.
+
+Messages require the expected window, exact HTTPS origin, random per-transfer token, validated payload, and a 60-second transfer window. Settings are never put into the URL. If popups or opener access are blocked (including by Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy), or storage is unavailable, use Download settings backup and Restore settings backup. The manual backup also works when an installed app reaches the new origin without opening the transfer page. Backups contain only the allowlisted keys; retain them privately until the move is complete. The old origin must remain reachable so users can recover settings.
+
+Run `pnpm test` for configuration, generated authorization, storage rollback, and origin validation checks. Run `pnpm build` after tests to regenerate artifacts with your chosen origins. No dev server is required.
