+# Daily Grind origin migration
+
+This repository starts as a static page, with no existing manifest, configuration
+store, deployment credentials, or origin addresses. The build prepares two static
+deployment bundles; it does not transfer a live installation by itself.
+
+```sh
+OLD_ORIGIN=https://www.example.com NEW_ORIGIN=https://coffee.example.com \
+OLD_APP_ID=/ CONFIG_KEYS='["daily-grind:preferences"]' npm run build
+npm test
+```
+
+Use your actual HTTPS origins. Chrome's native migration currently requires the
+origins to share the same registrable domain (eTLD+1). The build validates URL
+structure; the browser enforces this same-site restriction. Use the installed
+app's exact existing manifest ID as `OLD_APP_ID`. For an old manifest without an
+explicit ID, use its resolved `start_url` identity. Do not change that identity
+before migration. This template assumes the application is hosted at `/`.
+
+1. Review the generated manifests against the existing app: retain its name,
+   icons, display options, shortcut entries, and any other existing manifest
+   members. The sample includes a coffee icon and two application shortcuts.
+2. Deploy **dist/new/** to the new origin first. Its manifest explicitly declares
+   its ID and `migrate_from`, including an old-origin installation URL.
+3. Deploy **dist/old/** to the old origin, preserving the existing manifest's URL
+   (serve the generated old manifest at that URL if it differs from
+   `/manifest.webmanifest`). It includes `migrate_to` and
+   `/.well-known/web-app-origin-association` authorizing the destination app ID.
+4. Keep the old origin, manifest, association, `/migration-install.html`, and
+   `/migration-bridge.*` available with HTTP 200, without redirects or login walls.
+   Serve the association as `application/json` and the manifests as
+   `application/manifest+json`. Avoid stale caches for these migration resources.
+   If a service worker already exists, update it to serve these resources fresh.
+5. Keep `/migration-bridge.html` frameable **only by the destination origin**:
+   configure `Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors https://coffee.example.com`
+   on that response using your real destination. An inherited
+   `X-Frame-Options: DENY` or `SAMEORIGIN` will block preference transfer.
+
+The browser offers an installation update using `behavior: "suggest"`.
+Supported browsers migrate installation settings and OS integration through
+their native migration flow after the user accepts; JavaScript cannot rewrite
+OS shortcuts. No forced uninstall or automatic redirect is used. Browsers
+without native migration keep the old installation and receive a link to the
+new address, where installation remains a manual browser action.
+
+## User configuration
+
+Browser-managed installation settings and origin-local application data are
+separate. The destination requests only the `CONFIG_KEYS` allowlist from an
+old-origin iframe. Both sides check the exact origin and window; replies are
+bound to a fresh request nonce. Existing destination values win. Source data is
+never deleted. Set `CONFIG_KEYS` to the actual preference keys used by your app;
+the initial static page has no stored preferences. Do not include session tokens,
+credentials, or unrelated storage. Use `[]` if preferences are server-backed.
+
+Storage restrictions or partitioning can prevent the iframe from reading the
+original data. Failed or empty transfers show a retry option and a link back to
+the source, and are not marked complete. Cookies, authentication sessions,
+IndexedDB, caches, and service workers are not copied. Apps using those stores
+need their own authenticated data migration or account sync before retiring the
+old origin. Keep the source available until that work is complete.
+
+Native protocol and limitations:
+[Chrome's PWA origin migration documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/blog/seamless-pwa-origin-migration).
+
+Tests run in Node with isolated temporary output and simulated messaging/storage;
+they do not start servers or open a browser. The build has no runtime dependencies.
