@@ -2,6 +2,18 @@

 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.

+## Account settings and passkeys
+
+Account settings are available at `/devtools-times/account/settings`. Password sign-in still uses the existing Times identity service; successful sign-in now establishes an HttpOnly, same-site server session. Readers can register discoverable passkeys, sign in with them, rename their local labels, and remove them. The browser Signal API receives the complete accepted list on settings load and after removal, including an empty list after the last removal. Browsers without Signal API support show instructions to update the password manager manually. A label rename does not change the user's identity or the authenticator's account profile.
+
+Passkeys are verified with `@simplewebauthn/server`; public keys, provider AAGUIDs, counters, and raw registration/use timestamps are stored in SQLite. No sample credentials or users are seeded. The local provider registry is a subset of the [passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids); unknown and zero AAGUIDs use a generic key icon and label.
+
+Use Node.js 24 or newer. The default storage paths are `.data/accounts.sqlite` and `.data/sessions`. Set `ACCOUNT_DB_PATH` and `SESSION_DIR` to change them. Both must be writable and persistent. This filesystem configuration is intended for one host; deployments across hosts need shared account and session storage before enabling passkeys. Existing external passkeys are not imported automatically.
+
+In production, set `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN` to the exact public HTTPS origin (without a path or trailing slash) and `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID` to the domain used to register passkeys. If omitted, the server uses its request origin and hostname. The server supplies this RP ID to the client for synchronization. Keep the RP ID stable across deployments. Mutation endpoints enforce matching Origin headers; challenges expire after five minutes and are atomically consumed once. User verification is required for registration and passkey sign-in. Password sign-in remains available after the final passkey is removed.
+
+`pnpm test` runs isolated storage, account isolation, challenge expiry/replay, and browser synchronization tests without launching a server. `pnpm build` builds the app.
+

 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
