+# The Daily Grind
+
+A responsive homepage and passwordless customer account, using native WebAuthn in the browser, SimpleWebAuthn for server verification, and SQLite for accounts, passkeys, sessions, and one-time challenges.
+
+Requires Node.js 24 or newer and pnpm.
+
+```sh
+pnpm install
+pnpm build
+pnpm start
+```
+
+The default address is `http://localhost:11222`. The build command only creates frontend assets; the start command runs the application server and account API. Static hosting alone displays the homepage but cannot authenticate customers. The UI reports an unavailable account service rather than simulating authentication.
+
+## Configuration
+
+Set these environment variables in the process environment (the server does not load `.env` files):
+
+- `PORT`: HTTP listening port; defaults to `11222`.
+- `APP_ORIGIN`: exact public origin, including scheme and any nonstandard port; defaults to `http://localhost:${PORT}`.
+- `RP_ID`: WebAuthn relying party domain; defaults to the hostname of `APP_ORIGIN`. This must remain stable after enrolling credentials.
+- `DATA_DIR`: persistent SQLite directory; defaults to `./data`.
+- `NODE_ENV=production`: requires an HTTPS public origin and enables secure session cookies through the HTTPS origin configuration.
+
+In production, terminate HTTPS at a reverse proxy, set `APP_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS address, and mount `DATA_DIR` on persistent storage. The RP ID must match that origin's domain or a valid parent domain. Bind deployments to the intended public host; request `Origin` is checked against the configured value. Do not use a new origin or RP ID for existing passkeys.
+
+## Account flows
+
+- Register with a name and email, then create a discoverable passkey with device user verification. An account is saved only after successful registration verification.
+- Sign in from the passkey button or supported conditional autofill. Email is optional and, when supplied, must match the passkey account.
+- View, rename, add, and remove saved passkeys. Provider hints come from a bundled AAGUID registry; unknown providers get a generic icon. Provider information never determines account access.
+- Reauthenticate with an existing account-owned passkey before adding a key, removing a key, or saving profile changes. Authorization expires after two minutes and is consumed after a successful sensitive change.
+- Prevent removing the last passkey. Encourage a second password manager or security key for recovery.
+- Synchronize accepted credentials and updated profile details to supported password managers through feature-detected Signal APIs. Unsupported or failed background synchronization never blocks a successful account change.
+- Sign out by revoking the server session and clearing the HttpOnly cookie. Successful registration and sign-in rotate the session identifier.
+
+Email is an account identifier and contact field, not proof of mailbox ownership. This implementation deliberately does not offer email-only recovery: customers need a registered device, synced provider, or backup security key. Do not claim email verification or password recovery without adding those services.
+
+Passkeys require HTTPS or localhost, a compatible browser, and an available authenticator. Browser biometrics, private keys, and device PINs never reach the application. Server storage contains public keys and credential metadata only. Native JSON option parsing and serialization have local package polyfills; Signal APIs are optional enhancements.
+
+The small Node server is intended for a single application instance. Before scaling to several instances, use shared rate limiting and an appropriate shared database/session store. The local rate limiter does not trust forwarded IP headers.
+
+## Checks
+
+```sh
+pnpm test
+pnpm build
+```
+
+Tests call the API handler directly with in-memory SQLite and a software authenticator that creates real ES256 registration and assertion data. They cover successful account flows, counter updates, challenge replay/expiration/session binding, origin checks, invalid signatures, cross-account access, reauthentication enforcement, and protection of the last passkey. They do not start an HTTP server or browser.
+
+AAGUID metadata is a reduced snapshot of [passkey-authenticator-aaguids](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids), retaining provider names and small icons. Coffee photography is served by Unsplash; typography is served by Google Fonts.
