+# The Daily Grind
+
+Requires Node.js 22 or newer. `npm start` serves the homepage and newsletter API
+on port 11222 (override with `PORT`, optionally `HOST`). There are no dependencies
+or build step. `npm test` uses mocked requests; it does not open listening sockets.
+
+The form POSTs to `/api/newsletter`. Valid addresses are appended to
+`data/newsletter.jsonl` with a signup timestamp before success is returned.
+Use a persistent private volume in production, or set `NEWSLETTER_FILE` to a
+private writable file path. This stores subscriptions; newsletter delivery is a
+separate operational step. Subscriber files are excluded from Git and HTTP routes.
+
+Deploy behind HTTPS. Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the public HTTPS origin when using a
+reverse proxy (for example `https://dailygrind.example`) so same-origin POST
+validation uses the external origin. Forwarded headers are not implicitly trusted.
+
+Every response carries an enforced `Connection-Allowlist` allowing only the
+response origin and `https://images.unsplash.com`. Redirects and WebRTC retain
+their blocked defaults. CSP restricts scripts to the nonce-authorized local form
+script, styles to this server, images to this server and Unsplash, and form/API
+requests to this server. Workers and frames are blocked.
+
+Connection-Allowlist support varies by browser. CSP provides fetch, subresource
+and form restrictions elsewhere, but cannot fully reproduce the network-level
+protection for navigation, resource hints and WebRTC. The allowed Unsplash host
+is still a trusted destination, so the policy does not protect against misuse of
+that host or compromise of this server.
+
+Policy reports POST to `/api/security-reports`; only counts by violation type are
+logged. Customer emails, report URLs and script samples are never logged.
+Signup failures, policy blocks and 12-second timeouts show a retry message while
+keeping the email field intact. Native HTML submissions also receive a result
+page when JavaScript is unavailable.
