+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+The audit covered all application sources: the original index.html, server.js and
+package.json. No client-side scripts, DOM injection sinks, external scripts or
+stylesheets, frames, popup integrations, APIs, or browser permissions were present.
+The only external resource is the existing HTTPS Unsplash hero image. The old
+server returned the homepage for every request and its logout route did not clear
+any session state. No authentication backend or server session store exists.
+
+## Implemented protections
+
+- Extracted inline CSS and the heading's style attribute into styles.css. The page
+  needs no JavaScript. Script execution, inline event handlers and styles, plugin
+  content, base-tag overrides and framing are blocked by enforced CSP. Trusted
+  Types sink checks are required where supported, with no policy creation allowed.
+  Other browsers still receive CSP and script-free markup as the baseline defense.
+- CSP permits only local styles, the existing Unsplash image origin and local form
+  submissions. No unsafe-inline, unsafe-eval or broad script origin allowances.
+- All responses, including errors, carry nosniff, DENY, restrictive permissions,
+  no-referrer, CORP same-origin and COOP same-origin-allow-popups. COEP is omitted:
+  no shared-memory features require isolation, and external images must keep working.
+- Fetch Metadata rejects cross-origin subresource requests and mutations, including
+  sibling subdomains. Ordinary external links to documents still work. Missing
+  metadata is tolerated for older clients; logout also validates Origin if present.
+- Navigation logout is a native POST form and works without scripts. GET /logout
+  displays a confirmation form; HEAD is side-effect free. POST /logout returns a
+  no-store confirmation with Clear-Site-Data for cookies, storage and cache, plus
+  root-path expiry cookies (including HttpOnly session cookies and supplied names).
+  Clear-Site-Data requires HTTPS and browser support; cookies with other paths or
+  domains depend on that header. Its cookies directive also affects subdomains.
+  Future authentication must invalidate the server-side session/token before
+  returning success and expire cookies using their exact issuance attributes.
+- Only explicit public pages and styles.css are served. Existing navigation paths
+  retain the previous homepage behavior; unknown paths return 404. Request URLs
+  never resolve filesystem paths, and client Host values never enter HTML.
+
+## Rollout and reporting
+
+Start with `npm start`; PORT defaults to 11222. Node 18+ is required for the checks.
+Deploy behind a trusted HTTPS reverse proxy with NODE_ENV=production. Production
+responses enable HSTS with a short initial max-age=300; increase it only after TLS
+rollout succeeds. Subdomains and preload are deliberately not enrolled. Ensure the
+proxy preserves application security headers and does not cache no-store responses.
+
+The small, fully audited dependency-free page ships its CSP enforced immediately.
+Reporting-Endpoints and report-to point to /security-reports, with report-uri for
+older CSP clients. The collector accepts Reporting API batches and legacy CSP
+reports, limits bodies to 16 KiB and processing to 20 reports, and emits only fixed
+allowlisted directive names to stdout. URLs, referrers, user agents, script samples,
+cookies and raw report bodies are never logged. Apply request-rate limits at the
+reverse proxy before public deployment. Reports are untrusted telemetry, not an
+authentication mechanism. Reporting delivery and Clear-Site-Data must be served in
+a secure context.
+
+Before introducing new scripts, integrations, images, embeds or browser features,
+update the audit and add the required narrow permissions. For uncertain dependency
+changes, deploy a candidate Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only alongside the
+existing enforced policy, collect representative production traffic, triage real
+violations versus extensions and crawler noise, then promote the candidate. Do not
+relax the enforced policy with unsafe-inline or unsafe-eval. Approved future scripts
+need per-response cryptographic nonces or immutable hashes with strict-dynamic;
+untrusted text must use textContent and any HTML must be sanitized.
+
+## Validation
+
+`npm run build` syntax-checks server sources and runs handler-level regression tests
+with mock requests/responses. These cover policy/markup compatibility, public assets,
+logout and cookie expiration, cross-origin rejection, privacy-safe bounded reporting,
+HEAD/method handling, errors and production-only HSTS. Tests import request-handler.js,
+never server.js, and never bind a port. No browser or running server was used.
