@@ -0,0 +1,25 @@
+# Security audit and deployment
+
+The audit covered `index.html`, its CSS and resource URLs, the original `server.js`, and `package.json`. The site has no client-side JavaScript, authentication provider, session database, dependency packages, user-content rendering, third-party scripts, external navigation targets, or new-window links. Existing page URLs still display the shared homepage.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+- The old logout response only printed a message. The new POST `/logout` clears browser site data and expires root-path cookies; GET/HEAD show a sign-out form without changing state. The navigation uses a native POST form and works without JavaScript. Origin/Fetch Metadata checks reject cross-origin submissions, and speculative request markers are rejected. This prevents crawlers, prefetching, or prerendering from triggering logout via a link.
+- The inline stylesheet and inline heading style were moved to `/styles.css`. There are no inline event handlers, executable URLs, DOM injection sinks, or scripts to authorize. Scripts, eval, inline CSS, plugins, embedding, base URL changes, and outbound connections are blocked by CSP. Trusted Types adds protection against future DOM script injection in supporting browsers. Future scripts need an explicit policy review.
+- CSP permits only local styles and local/Unsplash images, preserving the existing stock photo. No wildcard or unsafe-inline exceptions are used. The HTTPS photo is the only external resource.
+- All application responses, including errors and logout, receive CSP, MIME sniffing protection, frame restrictions, referrer protection, permissions restrictions, opener/resource isolation, and `no-store`. DNS prefetch is disabled. File serving uses an explicit route allowlist so application source and repository files are never exposed.
+- HSTS is emitted for HTTPS deployments. It intentionally excludes subdomains and preload because ownership and HTTPS readiness of other hosts are unknown.
+
+## Running and rolling out
+
+`npm start` starts the dependency-free Node HTTP server on `PORT` (default `11222`). Terminate HTTPS at your deployment proxy and set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-domain.example` to the exact visitor-facing origin. This makes logout origin checks and Secure cookie deletion work behind that proxy. Do not configure an origin with a path. Arbitrary forwarded headers are not trusted. Keep the proxy's public routes and headers aligned with this server.
+
+CSP is enforced by default. For an initial observation phase, set `CSP_REPORT_ONLY=true`; inspect CSP violations in browser developer tooling during a separately authorized deployment check, then remove the variable to enforce. Report-only mode does not block CSP violations and has no remote report collector; the other security headers remain active. Do not add broad policy exceptions to silence violations. Review legitimate resource needs before extending the allowlist. CSP `frame-ancestors` requires enforcement; `X-Frame-Options: DENY` remains active during observation.
+
+Logout relies on browser support for the [Clear-Site-Data specification](https://www.w3.org/TR/clear-site-data/), which requires a secure context. Cookie clearing can also affect related subdomains, so deploy this site on a dedicated cookie domain if that isolation is required. The Set-Cookie fallback clears host-only cookies at `/`; cookies with other Domain/Path scopes require matching expiration attributes when authentication is integrated. There is currently no server-side session to revoke or identity-provider logout to call. Add both at the marked handler location before introducing real authentication; clearing browser state alone cannot invalidate a stolen token. Browsers without Clear-Site-Data support will only get the cookie fallback.
+
+The policy follows [CSP Level 3](https://www.w3.org/TR/CSP3/). Unsupported directives such as Trusted Types are ignored by older browsers; the other CSP restrictions still apply. No client script is needed for these protections.
+
+## Verification
+
+`npm run build` checks syntax without executing server code. `npm test` exercises the side-effect-free request handler with in-memory request/response objects and audits markup compatibility. It covers logout side effects and request provenance, speculative requests, security headers, HTTPS proxy configuration, report-only selection, route isolation, HTTP methods, and existing navigation URLs. Neither command starts a listening server, imports `server.js`, or opens a browser. Browser integration and deployment proxy behavior were not manually verified.
