@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+# SMS verification integration
+
+This repository serves a static homepage. `verification.js` provides the form and WebOTP lifecycle; real SMS delivery and authentication require the following same-origin backend endpoints. The UI never generates, stores, or accepts a code as proof of authentication locally.
+
+- `POST /api/auth/sms/request` receives JSON `{ "phone": "+12025550123", "purpose": "rewards" }` (or `"order"`). Return JSON `{ "challengeId": "opaque-server-generated-id" }` only after queuing the SMS. The UI enforces a 60-second resend cooldown; enforce rate limits on the server independently.
+- `POST /api/auth/sms/verify` receives JSON `{ "challengeId": "…", "code": "012345", "purpose": "rewards" }`. Validate the challenge and code on the server, establish a secure HttpOnly session cookie, then return `{ "verified": true }`. Only this response allows the client to navigate to `/rewards` or `/order-online`.
+- Return a non-2xx status for invalid/expired codes, failed delivery, and service errors; use 429 for rate limits. Do not return HTML or redirects from these JSON endpoints. Missing endpoints produce an honest service-unavailable message.
+
+Bind challenges to the phone, purpose, browser session, expiry, and attempt limit. Generate codes securely, expire them promptly, consume each once, and invalidate old codes on resend. Validate all payloads, enforce rate limits and same-origin/CSRF protections, and avoid account-enumerating responses. Serve over HTTPS. Do not log codes or full phone numbers.
+
+Use the actual serving hostname (no scheme, port, or path) on the final line of every SMS, with no signature or extra lines afterward:
+
+```text
+Your Daily Grind verification code is 012345.
+
+@your-production-hostname.example #012345
+```
+
+WebOTP starts before the send request and on each resend, and stops on submission, number/purpose changes, page exit, or timeout. Unsupported browsers and permission denials retain native `autocomplete="one-time-code"` suggestions and manual entry. There is one text input for the whole code, preserving leading zeros, paste, and screen reader navigation.
