--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# The Daily Grind
+
+This is a static page; no build is required. Run security regression checks with
+`npm test`.
+
+Configure `ORDERING_ORIGIN` and `SITE_DOMAIN` in `origin-validation.mjs` before
+deployment. The default trusted sender is `https://orders.dailygrind.coffee`;
+`dailygrind.coffee` is an explicitly owned registrable domain, not a suffix inferred
+from the deployment hostname. Never configure a public/shared suffix such as
+`co.uk` or `github.io` as the owned domain.
+
+The page accepts `{ type: 'status', text }` and `{ type: 'redirect', url }` messages
+from the exact trusted ordering origin. Status text is limited to 300 characters
+and rendered as text. Redirects allow only HTTP(S) URLs without credentials that
+pass a schemeful same-site check against the current page. The optional `returnTo`
+query parameter uses the same check to set the Order Now destination; it does not
+trigger automatic navigation. Invalid links cannot be activated, and external
+HTTP(S) links receive `noopener noreferrer`.
+
+The local ordering shortcut runs in an `allow-scripts` sandbox without
+`allow-same-origin`. Its first message must be `{ type: 'init' }` from that iframe's
+window. The page pins the actual opaque `Origin` from the browser message event
+and checks it on every subsequent message. Other iframe windows cannot initialize
+or replace the pin. A widget reload/navigation that changes its opaque origin
+requires reinitializing the parent page.
+
+Where `Origin` is unavailable, URL parsing provides exact origin validation.
+Same-site validation permits the current origin and, only when the page itself is
+on the configured owned domain, its same-scheme subdomains. Other deployment
+domains conservatively use exact-origin redirects. Widget validation pins the
+expected `event.source` window; these browsers cannot distinguish a new opaque
+document loaded into the same window. The native API path additionally rejects
+that origin change. Tests cover fallback behavior and native API wiring using a
+contract double; they do not claim browser-native Origin/PSL verification.
