+# Daily Grind
+
+The homepage includes a native rewards registration form and a Node 24+ backend.
+Install with `pnpm install`; `pnpm start` serves the homepage and verification endpoint.
+`pnpm build` checks syntax without starting a server. `pnpm test` runs in-memory
+request and cryptographic tests; it does not listen on a port or import server.mjs.
+
+## Deployment
+
+- Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the exact public HTTPS origin (no path). It is the trusted
+  key-binding audience and allowed POST origin; request Host headers are not trusted.
+- Register that origin for the [Email Verification origin trial](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification)
+  and set `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN` to the issued token. The homepage
+  sends it as an `Origin-Trial` response header. A real token cannot be bundled
+  before the deployment origin is known. Trial tokens may expire and need renewal.
+- Set `NODE_ENV=production` on deployment. Serve through HTTPS; HTTP localhost is
+  permitted for local development only.
+- Set `TRUSTED_ISSUER_ORIGINS` to a comma-separated list of approved issuer **and
+  JWKS** HTTPS origins. Defaults include Google issuer/key origins. Add other
+  participating providers after confirming their metadata and key hostnames.
+  Discovery requires matching email-domain DNS delegation and metadata issuer;
+  arbitrary token-provided hosts and discovery redirects are rejected.
+- Mount a persistent directory at `DATA_DIR` (default `./data`). Verified accounts
+  are stored in `rewards.sqlite` with unique email addresses, verification timestamps,
+  and an initial points balance of zero. Restrict access and back up this directory.
+
+## Registration behavior
+
+The server renders a fresh random nonce into the hidden
+`autocomplete="email-verification-token"` input on every homepage response, including
+retry pages. It retains the nonce and a separate CSRF token for ten minutes in
+server memory, keyed by an HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax session cookie (Secure on HTTPS).
+Pages use `Cache-Control: no-store`. The form uses ordinary browser submission so
+the browser can populate its proof at submission time, without client-side token
+handling or feature detection. JavaScript is not required.
+
+`POST /api/verify-email` consumes the session once, validates the fields, verifies
+DNS issuer delegation, issuer metadata, the EVT signature, verified email claim,
+expiry/freshness, and the browser's key-bound signature, nonce, audience, and token
+hash using `@sd-jwt/core` and `jose`. Only then is the rewards account saved.
+Repeated registrations keep the existing account unchanged. No email is sent.
+
+If the browser/provider does not support verification, permission is declined, the
+customer is signed out of the provider, or a proof fails validation, registration
+stays incomplete. The page preserves name/email and offers a new form with retry
+instructions; it never treats an unverified email as confirmed. No OTP or magic-link
+fallback is used, as required. The customer must use a supported browser/provider
+and approve browser verification to finish.
+
+This implementation is intended for a single Node process: sessions are in memory,
+so restarts invalidate outstanding forms. Use a shared session store with atomic
+nonce consumption before scaling across processes. Apply deployment-level request
+rate limits to the signup endpoint. Account sign-in, purchases, and reward redemption
+are outside this registration feature; email verification alone does not implement
+future account authentication.
