@@ -15,3 +15,43 @@ The application will now be running on `http://localhost:11224`.
 - **[React](https://react.dev/)**: Used for UI components.
 - **[Tailwind CSS](https://tailwindcss.com/)**: For styling the application.
 - **[Astro DB](https://docs.astro.build/en/guides/database/)**: Powers the comments feature, using a libSQL-compatible database.
+
+
+## Browser tools (WebMCP)
+
+The shared layout exposes client-side tools through `document.modelContext` in
+browsers with WebMCP enabled. Unsupported browsers keep the usual UI; no server
+transport or browser polyfill is needed. Tool definitions live in
+`src/scripts/browser-tools.ts`, with UI operations shared in
+`src/utils/article-client.ts`. The API is experimental; see the
+[WebMCP specification](https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/) and
+[Chrome declarative API documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/declarative-api).
+
+Available throughout the site:
+
+| Tool | Operation |
+| --- | --- |
+| `list_articles` | Retrieve article titles, summaries, and slugs. |
+| `search_articles` | Search titles and summaries with `query`. |
+| `get_reading_list` | Retrieve saved article slugs for the current session. |
+| `toggle_reading_list` | Add or remove an article using `slug`; updates mounted UI. |
+| `get_article_popularity` | Fetch popularity using an article `slug`. |
+| `get_tide_levels` | Fetch the coastal article's tide utility. |
+| `get_coffee_brew` | Fetch the coffee article's brewing utility. |
+| `get_plant_stats` | Fetch the plants article's statistics utility. |
+
+Opening Search or Reading List also exposes the corresponding annotated form
+(`search_articles_form` or `remove_reading_list_article_form`). Article pages
+expose `bookmark_article_form`. These forms use named native controls, normal
+submit handling, and `respondWith()` for agent-invoked submissions. Their actions
+are read-only or reversible, so they allow automatic submission. Reading-list
+removal is idempotent; repeating it cannot add the article back.
+
+Tools return actionable errors and utility tools preserve HTTP status and
+redirect information. Existing DevTools demonstration failures (such as coffee's
+500 response and the tide redirect) remain observable. Registration is scoped to
+Astro navigation and uses abort signals for cleanup and in-flight cancellation.
+
+Run `pnpm test` for mocked client-side registration, invocation, cancellation,
+and form-response checks (Node 22.13+ required). Run `pnpm build` for the production
+build. Neither command starts a server or performs manual browser verification.
